# environments
venv/
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/

# generated model outputs (reproduce with make run-v3 / run-legacy)
data/outputs/

# MATLAB run outputs (regenerated by run_all_scenarios on a licensed machine)
matlab/outputs/*
!matlab/outputs/.gitkeep
# ...EXCEPT artifacts downloaded from a real CI run. Those are RECEIVED EVIDENCE, not
# locally regenerable output: they are what MathWorks MATLAB actually produced, with the
# provenance the run wrote before anything executed. A commit claimed to have added them and
# had not, because `git add -A` silently honours this ignore -- the README then linked to a
# directory that existed on one laptop and nowhere else.
!matlab/outputs/run-*/
!matlab/outputs/run-*/**
matlab/simulink/
*.slxc
*.asv

# OS noise
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# local release bundles
release/
*.zip

# The 3D explorer's built web payload and GLB models are COMMITTED deliverables, not
# scratch output: the web tier must serve from a clean checkout with no build step, and
# tests/test_explorer_drift.py gates them against a fresh build. The standalone project
# ignored them, which was right for a scratch repo and wrong for a shipped one.
# Nothing under explorer/web/ is ignored -- this note exists so nobody re-adds it.

# Vendored third-party browser assets, fetched by scripts/vendor_cesium.sh.
# Build/Cesium is 20 MB across 375 files. It is pinned to the same release index.html
# loads and reproducible on demand, so committing it would add 20 MB to every clone of a
# planning-study repository for no determinism gain.
#
# It is NOT "bundled at desktop-packaging time" -- this comment used to say that, and no
# packaging step vendors it. `make explorer-vendor` is a manual, opt-in step; without it the
# viewer falls back to the pinned CDN, which is the documented behaviour.
explorer/web/vendor/

# Tableau build products that are NOT byte-reproducible.
#
# Measured, not assumed: two consecutive builds from a byte-identical spec produce an
# identical .twb and DIFFERENT .twbx digests, because the embedded Hyper extracts carry
# non-deterministic engine state. Tracking them would put a new binary in every commit that
# touches the workbook, and would tempt someone to gate on a digest that cannot hold.
#
# The .twb IS tracked and IS gated (tests/test_tableau_workbook.py), together with the sheet
# CSVs and the spec -- which is everything needed to rebuild.
dashboard/tableau/build/*.twbx
dashboard/tableau/build/Data/
# Machine-specific: the tracked workbook_spec.json holds relative paths; this variant has
# base_dir baked in for whichever machine ran the build.
dashboard/tableau/build/workbook_spec.build.json

# The provenance export is a generated artifact rebuilt from the contract. It is TRACKED,
# deliberately -- the web tier serves it and a reviewer must be able to diff what changed
# about a number's lineage. Listed here only to say so.

# Copied into the public bundle by scripts/build_platform_surfaces.py from an explicit
# allowlist. Generated, so not tracked -- but surfaces.json IS tracked, because a reviewer
# must be able to see what the bundle claims to contain.
site/platform/explorer/
site/platform/dashboard/
site/platform/contract/
site/platform/nfc/
